<h2>The prosecution narrative</h2>
<p>On 14 May 2025, a body was found in a park in Azadpur bearing multiple injuries to the head and face; the post-mortem recorded cranio-cerebral damage as the cause of death. CCTV footage placed three figures at the spot. Acting on a secret informer’s tip, the police identified them as the petitioner, a second adult, and a child in conflict with law. The petitioner was arrested the same day. The forensic science laboratory later reported that DNA extracted from blood on his T-shirt and jeans matched the deceased’s DNA. Charge under Section 103(1) read with Section 3(5) BNS was framed on 15 December 2025, and the trial has entered prosecution evidence, listed for 2 December 2026.</p>

<h2>The revision: grave suspicion is enough at charge</h2>
<p>The petitioner’s revision attacked the charge-framing order as mechanical. The Court found no illegality or perversity: arrest on the day of the incident, blood-stained clothing, and a DNA match together generated the grave suspicion the charge stage requires. Objections to the reliability of the CCTV identification, the informer’s information and the disclosure statements were held to be quintessential trial questions. The Court was careful to describe its observations as prima facie, not to prejudice the trial.</p>

<h2>The bail: how forensic evidence tilts the scale</h2>
<p>This was the third bail application. The petitioner pressed roughly seventeen months of custody and the principle — which the order itself acknowledges — that bail is the rule and jail the exception. The Court set against that the gravity of an offence punishable with death or imprisonment for life, the stage of the trial, and above all the FSL report. Quoting the Supreme Court’s formulation in <em>Sri Darshan</em> that the more heinous the crime, the greater the chance of refusal of bail, Justice Sachin Datta concluded that the incriminating forensic report was a conspicuous circumstance the Court was unable to ignore. The application was dismissed.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>The order is a useful illustration of how DNA evidence operates before trial: it is not proof of guilt — that assessment awaits cross-examination of the forensic witnesses — but at the bail stage it is weighty prima facie material connecting the accused to the offence, and it raises the bar for release considerably.</p>
</div>

<h2>The calibrated liberty to renew</h2>
<p>What keeps the order balanced is its final paragraph: the petitioner may renew his prayer for bail after the forensic witnesses have been examined, or if the trial does not progress materially within six months. The liberty does two things. It identifies, in advance, the change in circumstances that will make a fourth application maintainable rather than repetitive — the testing of the very evidence that defeated this one. And it builds in a speedy-trial safeguard: if the prosecution cannot move its own case forward, prolonged incarceration will itself become the new circumstance.</p>

<h2>For the practitioner</h2>
<p>Successive bail applications in Delhi survive or fail on demonstrated change — new evidence tested, witnesses turned, custody prolonged past identified milestones. Where an FSL report anchors the prosecution, the realistic strategy is usually the one this order maps: press for early examination of the forensic witnesses, build the record on chain of custody and sampling, and return to the bail court once that evidence has been tested rather than before.</p>
